  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe threat for thunderstorms has decreased over the past three\nhours this morning. Remnant showers are currently tracking south\nof I-70 along a shallow moisture boundary. Rainfall rates remain\nlight across northern West Virginia and do not currently pose a\nsignificant flooding threat. However, Flood and Flash Flood\nWarnings remain in effect for the next several hours for areas \nthat received over 2 inches of rain earlier and may continue to \nexperience excessive runoff. \n\nWith deep westerly flow across the Ohio River Valley, shower and\nthunderstorm redevelopment this afternoon will largely depend \non the evolution and remnants of mesoscale convective systems \n(MCSs) currently moving across Indiana and Illinois. The threat\nfor training showers and thunderstorms capable of producing \nadditional flooding is expected to remain south of our forecast \nregion. \n\nFor our area, the primary concern will be lightning from\nisolated thunderstorms between 2pm and 7pm this afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMachine-learning models suggest that after a brief period of \nridging on Friday, our large-scale weather pattern will \ntransition to a more zonal flow. As a result, this will allow \ndisturbances forming upstream (to our west) to track into our \narea. This active pattern could support heavy rain and/or \nsevere weather threats early next week and will need to be \nmonitored.",
